 'F1': {'path':'schemas/af_wcc_vacuum.yaml','sha256':'9a8b...','class_id':'AF-WCC-VAC-GEN'},
 'F2a': {'path':'schemas/af_scc_c2_vacuum.yaml','sha256':'b612...','class_id':'AF-SCC-C2-VAC-GEN'},
 'F2b': {'path':'schemas/af_scc_c0_vacuum.yaml','sha256':'1bb7...','class_id':'AF-SCC-C0-VAC-GEN'},
}
TAXONOMY = {'path':'research_map/formulation_taxonomy.yaml','sha256':'2760...','class_id':'GLOBAL'}
LOGICAL = {'forall','exists','not','and','or','in','subset','iff','implies','with','letting','be','the','a','of','is','that','such'}
```

Tokenization: I need to handle macros. Approach: replace `_{...}` and `^{...}` with `_SUB`/`_SUP` placeholders before tokenizing, but preserve the full macro token for reporting. Simpler robust method:

```python
def normalize(sf):
    # join subscript/superscript brace groups into the preceding identifier
    s = sf
    s = re.sub(r'([A-Za-z0-9_]+)_\{([^{}]*)\}', lambda m: m.group(1)+'_{'+m.group(2)+'}', s)  # already
```
Actually the issue: regex `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` on "AF_{I+}" yields "AF_" then "{I+}". I'll write a custom tokenizer:

```python
TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:_\{[^{}]*\}|\^\{[^{}]*\})?(?:_[A-Za-z0-9]+)?")
